Your own deny patterns did not block bash when you passed --yes or answered "always". That's fixed and committed as 64f88a4e, and fmt, clippy (-D warnings) and the full cargo test all pass.

What was broken: I wrote the --yes test before changing anything, and it was red. yoyo --yes --deny "echo BLOCKED_ZZ" ran the command and returned Exit code: 0\nBLOCKED_ZZ. Your deny list was only checked inside the bash prompt step, which fails three ways:
1. **--yes:** the prompt step is never set up, so your deny list was never checked.
2. "always": after you press a once, every later command is approved before your deny list is looked at.
3. **Commands the safety checker warns about:** the deny list was compared against the warning text (`⚠️ …\nCommand: …`) instead of the command itself. Patterns must match from the start, so `deny = ["git push --force*"]` showed a prompt instead of refusing.

I found the third one while reading the code; the task didn't mention it. Only the first was shown red against the old code. The other two come from reading the code, and the old code had no way to set the "always" state from a test.

The fix (`src/tools.rs`): your deny list is now checked in one place, inside the bash tool, against the command exactly as written, before any prompt or auto-approval. The prompt step now only sees your allow list, so deny isn't checked in two places. allow behaves as before, and --yes still skips the prompt for commands no deny pattern matches.

Tests (`src/tools_user_deny_tests.rs`): three tests, one per way above. Each checks that the error names the pattern. The --yes and "always" tests also check that echo ok_ZZ still runs. The warning-path test checks that a denied command never reaches the prompt. It uses a prompt that always says no and a throwaway directory, so even a broken build can't run git push --force from the repo.

Check that the tests catch the bug: I disabled the new check in one command and all 3 tests failed by name. I restored it in the same command and they pass, with no disable markers left in the code.

Other changes:
• **Size limit:** `src/tools.rs` went past its recorded size limit, so the tests are in their own file, following the existing `*_tests.rs` convention. The limit in tests/module_size.rs now records 4940 → 4975 (+35) and why.
• **Docs:** `docs/src/configuration/permissions.md` said `--yes` approves "everything" without mentioning your deny list. It now says a deny pattern still blocks bash under --yes and after "always".
• **CHANGELOG.md:** one entry describing the before-and-after.

Still open: write_file/edit_file look like they skip your deny list after "always" in the same way (confirm_file_operation in src/tool_wrappers.rs). This task was bash-only, so I left them alone, and the CHANGELOG says file-path deny is unchanged. That probably deserves its own task.
